    <item>
      <title>Importing Kindle Clippings in Emacs</title>
      <link>https://stewart123579.github.io/blog/posts/emacs/importing-kindle-clippings-in-emacs/</link>
      <pubDate>Thu, 04 Sep 2025 00:00:00 +1000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://stewart123579.github.io/blog/posts/emacs/importing-kindle-clippings-in-emacs/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;I wrote &lt;a href=&#34;https://github.com/stewart123579/ebook-notes&#34;&gt;ebook-notes.el&lt;/a&gt;, an Emacs Lisp package, to streamline the process of importing highlights and notes from an Amazon Kindle’s “My Clippings.txt” file directly into Org mode files. It automatically handles the association of notes with their corresponding highlights and prevents the import of duplicate entries.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To make life interesting, I decided to try using a LLM to &lt;em&gt;&amp;ldquo;help&amp;rdquo;&lt;/em&gt;.  I used Google&amp;rsquo;s Gemimi 2.5 Flash model.  Don&amp;rsquo;t judge me.  This was &lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;research&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/em&gt;!&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
